1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,365
Good morning everyone.

2
00:00:01,365 --> 00:00:06,675
Now whether you're going to university to study business or some other subject, many

3
00:00:06,675 --> 00:00:11,780
of you will eventually end up working for a company of some kind.

4
00:00:11,780 --> 00:00:11,830
Now,

5
00:00:11,830 --> 00:00:18,513
when you first start working somewhere you will realise that the organisation you've joined

6
00:00:18,513 --> 00:00:19,860
has certain characteristics.

7
00:00:19,860 --> 00:00:26,942
And we often refer to these social characteristics as the culture of the organisation-this

8
00:00:26,942 --> 00:00:32,000
includes its unwritten ideas, beliefs, values and things like that.

9
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:39,640
One well known writer has classified company cultures by identifying four major types.

10
00:00:39,640 --> 00:00:43,373
The first type is called the Power Culture,

11
00:00:43,373 --> 00:00:46,640
and it's usually found in small organisations.

12
00:00:46,640 --> 00:00:51,622
It's the type of culture that needs a central source of power to be

13
00:00:51,622 --> 00:00:51,978
effective,

14
00:00:51,978 --> 00:00:58,028
and because control is in the hands of just one or two people there aren't many rules

15
00:00:58,028 --> 00:00:58,740
or procedures.

16
00:00:58,740 --> 00:01:07,506
Another characteristic is that communication usually takes the form of conversations rather than, say, formal meetings

17
00:01:07,506 --> 00:01:09,150
or written memos.

18
00:01:09,150 --> 00:01:14,146
Now one of the benefits of this culture is that the organisation has the

19
00:01:14,146 --> 00:01:15,573
ability to act quickly,

20
00:01:15,573 --> 00:01:17,715
so it responds well to threat,

21
00:01:17,715 --> 00:01:19,856
or danger on the one hand,

22
00:01:19,856 --> 00:01:21,640
and opportunity on the other.

23
00:01:21,640 --> 00:01:26,538
But on the negative side, this type of organisation doesn't always act effectively,

24
00:01:26,538 --> 00:01:31,436
because it depends too much on one or two people at the top,

25
00:01:31,436 --> 00:01:36,710
and when these people make poor decisions there's no-one else who can influence them.

26
00:01:36,710 --> 00:01:41,357
And the kind of person who does well in this type of business culture

27
00:01:41,357 --> 00:01:44,013
is one who is happy to take risks,

28
00:01:44,013 --> 00:01:47,000
and for whom job security is a low priority.

29
00:01:47,000 --> 00:01:53,300
The next type is known as Role Culture-that's R-O-L-E, not R-O-double L, by the

30
00:01:53,300 --> 00:01:53,750
way,

31
00:01:53,750 --> 00:01:57,800
and this type is uaually found in large companies,

32
00:01:57,800 --> 00:02:01,400
which have lots of different levels in them.

33
00:02:01,400 --> 00:02:07,527
These organisations usually have separate departments that specialise in things like finance,

34
00:02:07,527 --> 00:02:08,548
or sales,

35
00:02:08,548 --> 00:02:09,569
or maintenance,

36
00:02:09,569 --> 00:02:10,590
or whatever.

37
00:02:10,590 --> 00:02:16,693
Each one is co-ordinated at the top by a small group of senior managers,

38
00:02:16,693 --> 00:02:21,052
and typically everyone's job is controlled by sets of rules

39
00:02:21,052 --> 00:02:26,282
and procedures - for example, there are specific job descriptions, rules for discipline,

40
00:02:26,282 --> 00:02:27,590
and so on.

41
00:02:27,590 --> 00:02:30,750
What are the benefits of this kind of culture?

42
00:02:30,750 --> 00:02:31,583
Well firstly,

43
00:02:31,583 --> 00:02:37,411
because it's found in large organisations, its fixed costs or over head as they

44
00:02:37,411 --> 00:02:43,240
are known are low in the relation to its outputs or what it produces.

45
00:02:43,240 --> 00:02:47,400
In other words it can achieve economies of scale.

46
00:02:47,400 --> 00:02:54,630
And secondly, it is particularly successful in business markets where technical expertise is important.

47
00:02:54,630 --> 00:03:00,692
On the other hand, this culture is often very slow to recognise the need

48
00:03:00,692 --> 00:03:01,125
forchange,

49
00:03:01,125 --> 00:03:03,290
and even slower to react.

50
00:03:03,290 --> 00:03:06,620
What kind of person does this type of culture suit?

51
00:03:06,620 --> 00:03:10,017
Well it suits employees who value security,

52
00:03:10,017 --> 00:03:13,900
and who don't particularly want to have responsibility.

53
00:03:13,900 --> 00:03:21,500
Moving on now to Task Cultures-this type is found in organistations that are project-oriented.

54
00:03:21,500 --> 00:03:26,808
You usually find it where the market for the company's product is extremely competitive

55
00:03:26,808 --> 00:03:30,220
or where the products themselves have a short life-span.

56
00:03:30,220 --> 00:03:36,170
Usually top management delegates the projects, the people and other resources.

57
00:03:36,170 --> 00:03:42,018
And once these have been allocated, little day-to-day control is exercised from the top,

58
00:03:42,018 --> 00:03:45,360
because this would seem like 'breaking the rules'.

59
00:03:45,360 --> 00:03:49,910
Now one of the major benefits of this culture is that it's flexible.

60
00:03:49,910 --> 00:03:53,290
But it does have some major disadvantages too.

61
00:03:53,290 --> 00:03:59,500
For instance, it can't produce economies of scale or great depth of expertise.

62
00:03:59,500 --> 00:04:04,750
People who like working in groups or teams prefer this type of culture.

63
00:04:04,750 --> 00:04:10,000
And finally, the fourth category is called the Person Culture…
